From c650004fe5a60025d3595c801fb89e24d83fe7a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:27:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] M10: the sound of the game (GDD 16.5): generated buffers,
 loops by the snapshot, the mix

Every sound of the table is made at boot from its entry in data/sfx.json.
SfxSynth turns a "synth" entry into a 16-bit AudioStreamWAV: a wave (sine,
saw, square, noise), its pitch (a frequency that may glide or trill, voices
together, or a row of notes and rests), a filter that may sweep, and how
its loudness moves (ramps, decay, bursts). The same entry says how the
sound is played: how loud, once or as a loop, how often it repeats. A file
named by "stream" is played in its place. An entry with a key nothing
reads, or one that means nothing for its sound, is an error at boot.

Audio plays every sfx_requested, a hit (scrape or crash), the rising squeal
of a close-call chain and the ding of a heckle. Audio.apply(snapshot),
called once a frame by the drive scene, keeps what lasts: the engine (its
pitch by the speed, a different added harmonic in each gear), the rumble
strip, the heartbeat of Pound, the radio's station or static or the tape,
the siren's chirps, the ringing phone, the indicator's tick, the Phantom's
hum, the horn held through a Snap. The sound moves on by the snapshot's own
clock and falls silent when the snapshots stop.

The mix: the rumble strip, the indicator tick and both yawns play on a bus
of their own, and while one of them sounds everything else is turned down.

A loop is written with its first samples again after its end: Godot reads
on past a loop's end as it turns, and without them the loops of the car's
engine and of the hum clicked on every turn.

tools/audio_check.gd makes and measures every buffer (length, peak, RMS),
compares the entries with the table of GDD 16.5, weighs each functional
cue against everything else at once, and takes Audio through the states of
a drive and every sound the simulation asks for. tools/check.sh runs it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
